-- title:
--   Toric points in a Hecke corner and its Weil annihilator
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $p$ is a prime with $p \neq 2$ (hypothesis `hp2`) and $M$ a positive integer with $p \mid M$ and $p^2 \nmid M$ (hypotheses `hpM`, `hpM2`, so that $M/p$ is again nonzero), $H$ is a subgroup of $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ which, by `hHp`, contains every unit whose image under the reduction $(\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times \to (\mathbb{Z}/(M/p))^\times$ is $1$, and $S$ is a set of natural numbers. The hypothesis `hin` is [`ModularCurve.HeckeDiamondInputsHAll M H`](def/ModularCurve_XHOperators.html#L113): for every prime $\ell$ the Hecke inputs `HeckeInputsHAlong` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ at level $(M,H)$ and $\ell$ hold, and every $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$ is realised by some $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism $\sigma$ of $\overline{X_H(M)}$'s function field `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` satisfying `IsDiamondAutHBar M H d σ`. Here `JH M H` denotes $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ of that function field over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, and [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) its $p$-adic Tate module, realised as the group of sequences $(x_n)$ with $p^n x_n = 0$ and $p x_{n+1} = x_n$.
--
--   Hecke data: $\mathbb{T}$ is a commutative ring, an algebra over $\mathbb{Z}_p$, acting on [`TateModule p (JH M H)`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L15) compatibly with the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-action; `hfaith` says this action is faithful; `op` assigns to each generator $g$ of [`CohCarrier.Gen M S`](def/CohCarrier_Inst.html#L13) (the symbols $T_\ell$ for primes $\ell \notin S$ with $\ell \nmid M$, $U_q$ for primes $q \mid M$, and $\langle d \rangle$ for $d \in (\mathbb{Z}/M)^\times$) an element of $\mathbb{T}$ which by `hop` acts on the Tate module as `tateGenOpH M H S p g`; and `hgen` says that the $\mathbb{Z}_p$-subalgebra generated by the range of `op` is all of $\mathbb{T}$. Further, $S'$ is an idempotent splitting of $\mathbb{T}$, that is, a finite family of idempotents $e_i$ and maximal ideals $\mathfrak{m}_i$ ($i \in \mathrm{Fin}\,S'.n$) which are completely orthogonal, exhaust the maximal ideals, and satisfy $e_i \in \mathfrak{m}_j \iff i \neq j$; an index $i_0$ is fixed, and `hord` requires that the image of the generator $U_p$ under `op` does not lie in $\mathfrak{m}_{i_0}$.
--
--   Geometric data: `Pl` is a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ lying over $p$ in the sense that $p$ is a non-unit of `Pl` (`hPl`), with residue field of characteristic $p$ and algebraically closed; `hj` places the $q$-expansion `jqModC ℚ` of $j$ in the function field `qExpFunctionFieldC ℚ ⊤`; $\mathfrak{X}$ is an integral model `XHDRModelAtP p M H hpM hj` of $X_H(M)$ over the base ring `R p`, carrying in particular a curve model `𝔛.Meta` of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, the comparison isomorphism `𝔛.eeta` with the geometric fibre, the cusp section `𝔛.εinf`, the degeneracy morphism `𝔛.π` and the involution `𝔛.w`. Next, $\Lambda$ is level data `JHNeronObjectAtP.LevelData p M H hpM Pl` (a structure morphism $\sigma_A$, a scheme $\Lambda.X$ with morphism $\Lambda.f$ to `base p = Spec (baseRing p)`, where `baseRing p` is the subring of $\mathbb{Q}$ of rationals with denominator coprime to $p$, a relative group law and parametrisations of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ at level $(M/p, \mathrm{infSubgroup}\,p\,M\,H)$ generically and on the special fibre), $h\Lambda$ asserts that $\Lambda.f$ is smooth and proper with connected fibres and admits a relative group law, and $O$ is a Néron object `JHNeronObjectAtP p M H hpM Pl hPl Λ`, whose data include a scheme $O.G$ with structure morphism $O.g$ over `base p`, a commutative relative group law $O.L$, a bijection $O.\mathrm{pts}$ from `JH M H` onto the points of $O.g$ over the geometric generic point `genPt p`, Hecke endomorphisms, and the toric subgroups $O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,m$, defined for $m>0$ as the subgroup of `JH M H` generated by the toric points $O.\mathrm{toricPoint}\,m$.
--
--   Abel–Jacobi and representability hypotheses (named here, their content summarised): `hD` asserts that the relative Picard condition `algEquivZeroCut` — rigidified line bundles on the model `toBase p (ΓM M H) hj` rigidified along `𝔛.εinf` whose geometric fibres are algebraically equivalent to zero — is represented by the designation built from $O.G$, $O.g$ and the unit point of $O.L$ over the identity of `Spec (R p)`; `hDQ` asserts the corresponding representability after base change to $\mathbb{Q}$, by the base change of that designation; `hsep` says the base-changed curve is separated; `ajQ` is a morphism over $\mathbb{Q}$ from the base-changed curve to the base-changed designation, `kQ` a comparison morphism between the pullbacks of the model along `genPt p` and along `specMap (R p) ℚ`, pinned by `hkQ₁`, `hkQ₂` to be compatible with the two projections and with $\mathrm{Spec}$ of $\mathbb{Q} \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$; `hpoinc` identifies the Poincaré bundle of `hDQ` with the base change to $\mathbb{Q}$ of the pullback of the Poincaré bundle of `hD`; `hajQε` says `ajQ` carries the base-changed cusp section to the zero section; `hajQ` is the universal Abel–Jacobi property of `ajQ`: for every field $K$, every $K$-point $t$ of $\mathrm{Spec}\,\mathbb{Q}$ and every point $x$ of the base-changed curve over $t$, the pullback of the Poincaré bundle along $x$ followed by `ajQ` is isomorphic to the line bundle of the relative effective Cartier divisor of $x$ tensored with the ideal module of the divisor of the cusp section at $t$. Then `ajbar : 𝔛.Meta.C ⟶ O.G` is, by `hajbar`, the composite of `𝔛.eeta`, `kQ`, `ajQ` and the first projection of the pullback of $O.g$ along `specMap (R p) ℚ`; `hajbar_over` records that `ajbar` followed by $O.g$ equals `𝔛.Meta.toBase` followed by `genPt p`; `εbar` is a geometric point of `𝔛.Meta.C` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ which by `hεbar` lies over the cusp section and by `hεbar_aj` is carried by `ajbar` to the unit point of $O.L$; `hpts_law` says that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ is additive for the relative group law furnished by `hD`; and `hAJ` says that for all geometric points $x, s$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` with $s$ lying over the cusp section there is a degree-zero divisor $D_v$ on the function field equal to $\delta_{v(x)} - \delta_{v(s)}$, where $v(\cdot)$ denotes the place attached to a point by `𝔛.Meta.pointEquivPlace`, such that $O.\mathrm{pts}$ of the class of $D_v$ is $x$ followed by `ajbar`. Finally `hrepΛ` asserts that the analogous representability holds at the level `ΓN p M H hpM`, with the section `𝔛.εinf` followed by `𝔛.π`, represented by the designation built from $\Lambda.X$, $\Lambda.f$ and the unit point of $\Lambda.L$.
--
--   Involution data: `wgen` is a semilinear automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, i.e. a pair of ring automorphisms of the function field and of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the structure map, and `hwgen` says that `wgen` implements the involution `𝔛.w` on places: whenever two geometric points $y, y'$ of `𝔛.Meta.C` satisfy that $y'$ followed by `𝔛.eeta`, the first projection and `𝔛.w.hom` agrees with $y$ followed by `𝔛.eeta` and the first projection, then the place of $y'$ is `wgen` acting on the place of $y$. Moreover $\theta$ is a $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$-algebra automorphism of `xHFunctionFieldBar M H` which by `hθ` acts on $q$-expansions as the degeneracy in $p$: if $f$ at level $(M,H)$ and $u$ at level $(M/p, \mathrm{infSubgroup}\,p\,M\,H)$ have the same Laurent series, then the Laurent series of $\theta f$ is `qExpand` by $p$ applied to that of $u$; and `hwθ` identifies `wgen` with the semilinear automorphism induced by $\theta$.
--
--   Pairing data: the function field has principal divisors of degree zero for every nonzero element, $e$ is a divisorial Weil pairing datum of level $p$, that is, a pairing on the $p$-torsion of $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ with values in $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ compatible with the pairing of every Weil datum and satisfying the moving property for supports; and $B : \mathrm{JH}\,M\,H \times \mathrm{JH}\,M\,H \to \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ is any function which by `hB` agrees with $e.\mathrm{pair}$ on pairs of classes annihilated by $p$.
--
--   Conclusion. Write $\mathrm{pr}_1$ for [`TateModule.proj p (JH M H) 1`](def/EllipticCurve_TateModule.html#L122), and for $f \in \mathbb{T}$ let $\mathrm{corner}(f)$ denote the $\mathbb{T}$-submodule $f \cdot \mathrm{T}_p(\mathrm{JH}\,M\,H)$, the range of multiplication by $f$ on the Tate module. Then the two sets
--   $$\{\, x \in \mathrm{JH}\,M\,H \;:\; x \in \mathrm{pr}_1(\mathrm{corner}(S'.e\,i_0)) \ \text{ and } \ x \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p \,\}$$
--   and
--   $$\{\, y \in \mathrm{JH}\,M\,H \;:\; p\,y = 0, \ B(x,y) = 1 \text{ for all } x \in \mathrm{pr}_1(\mathrm{corner}(1 - S'.e\,i_0)), \ \text{ and } \ y \in O.\mathrm{toricPts}\,p \,\}$$
--   have the same `Set.ncard`: the toric points in the corner cut out by the idempotent $e_{i_0}$ are exactly as many as the $p$-torsion toric points which are $B$-orthogonal to the complementary corner.
--
--   This is the counting symmetry between a Hecke corner of $J_H(M)[p]$ and the $B$-annihilator of the complementary corner, taken on the toric part of the Néron object at a place above $p$; it is the form in which self-duality of the Weil pairing under the Hecke action, together with the Atkin–Lehner involution at $p$, enters the analysis of the toric part in Ribet-style level lowering. It is used in the derivation of the perfect-pairing and cyclotomic-toric-count statements for $J_H(M)$ at $p$ and in the product formula relating the toric and finite parts of the corner.
